When the AZTX folded in 1933 Syd was able to join the PCL, pitching for the Portland Beavers. When released by Portland he returned to El Paso and finished out the summer playing for local semipro clubs. Late in the year Cohen received another chance in the long-season PCL, engaged as a relief pitcher by the Hollywood Stars. Combined with his Portland numbers, he posted an unimpressive 1-3 record overall, but batted (9-for-26) .346. 

In 1934 Cohen joined the Minneapolis Millers of the Class AA American Association as a pitcher but was used in the outfield and first base as needed.  The engagement occasioned a Cohen family reunion, enabling him to play with his brother Andy, the Millers second baseman.    That September, Syd was traded to the Chattanooga Lookouts of the Class A Southern Association, an affiliate of the Washington Senators.   Prior to being called up to the big club Syd filled in for injured Mule Shirley at first basebase, when not pitching. Shortly thereafter, Cohen was called up to the big club. 
Syd Cohen made his major league debut on September 18, 1934 as an outfielder in the second game of a doubleheader in Cleveland, replacing Heine Manush who needed a rest. Batting lefty against Indians reliever Ralph Winegarner, Cohen singled in his second at at-bat, driving in a run. Otherwise went hitless batting from both sides of the plate but played errorless left field defense in a 9-6 loss.   Two days later, Syd made his pitching debut throwing a complete game but suffering a 6-1 defeat to Cleveland.   
In the final series to end the season against the New York Yankees Al Schacht was the acting manager for the final games of the season while Joe Cronin celebrated his honeymoon.
Acting Manager Al Schacht, when Joe Corin was on his honeymoon, announced that Syd would pitch the final game of the season. On September 29, 1934, Syd filled in for Reese Diggs who was late for his start against the Yankees, making history as the pitcher of record for Babe Ruth's last home run and strikeout in a Yankee uniform.  Cohen went the distance, spacing 11 hits and four walks well enough to emerge with an 8-5 victory. Later in life Cohen reflected back on the game saying:
“I was awed when I watched those great sluggers slam ball after ball over the fence in batting practice.  It scared me just to look at the Babe, whom I had never seen before. But I was secretly glad I was going to pitch against him.”  
