To prepare for the 1937 season, Cohen played on various El Paso sandlot baseball clubs to learn how to throw a screwball.    During Senators spring training he showed off his screwball,      stating that “I have confidence in that pitch.”   He also began throwing a submarine curve.
Used exclusively in relief – his one start was rained out after four innings with Washington ahead, 4-0 – Cohen went 2-4, with a commendable 3.11 ERA in 55 innings pitched for the 1937 Senators. Season highlights included 1937 is his first full season with the Senators, primary as a relief pitcher. His only start was a rainout game against Cleveland that ended in the fourth inning with Senators leading 4-0.      Replacing Carl Fisher in the seventh inning of June 18 and hit a single off reliever young Bob Feller   the future HOF pitcher.   At the end of the season, the sale of Cohen’s contract to the International League Baltimore Orioles brought his brief major league career to a close. Over parts of three Washington campaigns, he posted a 3-7 (.300) record, with a 4.54 ERA in 109 innings thrown. Over that span, he surrendered 133 base hits and 37 walks while striking out 49. With the stick, Cohen batted a meek (5-for-32) .152 but fielded his position capably with a .980 FA in his 55 mound appearances. 
 
