When Major League Commissioner Ford Frick expressed interest in creating a third major league in 1954,  Syd wrote him a letter that started: “Please consider this an application on behalf of the El Paso baseball club for a franchise in any third major league which may be organized in the future along such lines as outlined by the commissioner at the major league meeting in New York.”   
Midway through the 1955 baseball season Syd was promoted to business manager of the El Paso Texans, now in the Class B West Texas-New Mexico League. He also assumed the manager’s post when skipper Jim Mangan left the team in mid-August.   The Spanish-fluent Cohen then spent the next two seasons managing the Ciudad Juarez club of the Central Mexican League.      
Sometime between 1957 and 1958, Syd took a job working for the Coors Brewing Company as a sales representative, a position [role] that he retained [kept] even while volunteering as pitching coach for the Texas Western College, now the University of Texas at El Paso.  In 1963, George Genovese, field manager for the El Paso Sun Kings, engineered a rule change to permit the Cohen brothers to work with players at home games. Syd tutored pitchers while Andy coached first base.     Meanwhile in March [300 El Pasoans witness] Syd was [’s] inducted into the El Paso Athletic Hall of Fame.   That same year, Andy and Syd saw the Texas Western College baseball team play its first game.  Andy started up and managed the new baseball program, while Syd served  as pitching coach, [a] volunteer positions that the brothers held until 1978.    
On April 9, 1988, Syd Cohen died from cancer cancer at the age of 81. Ray Sanchez said, “I’m not ashamed to say I wept when I learned that Syd had died.  I’m sure many others did the same.”  Following funeral services, Cohen was interred at B’Nai Zion Cemetery in El Paso. To preserve the memory of Syd and brother Andy, El Paso opened Cohen Stadium in June 1990. The edifice served as a local testament until razed in 2019. 
